# =============================================================================
# Environment template for the CRM TimeSheet project.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` in the same directory and replace the placeholder
# values. `.env` is gitignored and must NEVER be committed.
#
#   Windows (PowerShell):  Copy-Item .env.example .env
#   Linux / macOS:         cp .env.example .env
#
# Docker Compose reads `.env` automatically because it sits next to
# `docker-compose.yml`. The NestJS backend reads it through @nestjs/config.
#
# The backend validates the variables it uses at startup and refuses to boot
# when one is missing or malformed — see
# `backend/src/config/env.validation.ts`.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PostgreSQL — consumed by the `postgres` service in docker-compose.yml.
#
# IMPORTANT: POSTGRES_USER, POSTGRES_PASSWORD and POSTGRES_DB are read by the
# postgres entrypoint on FIRST boot only, when it initialises the data
# directory. Changing them later has no effect while the named volume
# `crm-timesheet-postgres-data` still exists — see README "Data persistence".
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Required. Database superuser created on first boot.
POSTGRES_USER=crm_user

# Required. Password for POSTGRES_USER. Use a strong value; never commit it.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=replace_with_strong_password

# Required. Default database created on first boot.
POSTGRES_DB=crm_timesheet

# Optional. Host port the container's 5432 is published on, bound to
# 127.0.0.1 only. Change it if a local PostgreSQL install already owns 5432.
# Defaults to 5432 when unset.
POSTGRES_PORT=5432

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Application
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Required by the backend. PostgreSQL connection string.
# Host is `localhost` because the backend runs on the host during development.
# It becomes `postgres` (the Compose service name) once the backend itself
# runs as a Compose service. Keep user/password/db in sync with the values
# above, and with POSTGRES_PORT if you changed it.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://crm_user:replace_with_strong_password@localhost:5432/crm_timesheet?schema=public"

# Optional. Runtime environment for the backend. One of `development`, `test`
# or `production`. Defaults to `development`.
NODE_ENV=development

# Optional. HTTP port the NestJS backend binds to. Integer between 1 and 65535.
# Defaults to 3000.
PORT=3000

# Optional. Comma-separated list of browser origins allowed to call the API
# (CORS). Each origin must match scheme + host + port exactly — no trailing
# slash, no path:
#
#   CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173,https://crm.example.com
#
# `*` allows any origin; credentials are then disabled automatically, because
# browsers reject credentialed requests against a wildcard.
#
# When unset or empty, no browser origin is allowed — the safe default, so a
# misconfigured environment fails closed. Non-browser clients (curl, Postman,
# server-to-server calls) send no Origin header and are never affected.
#
# The value below is the Vite dev server default, i.e. the local frontend.
CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database seeding — read by `backend/prisma/seed.ts` only, never by the
# running application.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Optional. Password given to every account created by `npm run prisma:seed`.
# When unset, the seed uses the development default documented in
# `FEATURES/005-database-seeding.md`, which is published in this repository.
#
# Set it to anything else whenever the database is reachable by someone other
# than you — a shared development server, a demo environment, a teammate's
# machine. The seed refuses to run at all when NODE_ENV=production.
#
# Changing it only affects accounts the seed creates. Existing accounts keep
# their current password; `npm run prisma:reset` recreates them from scratch.
# SEED_PASSWORD=
